Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs Markdown — Complementary, not alternatives
	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Last modif: 2026-09-01
		Description: Markdown formats prose; STXT structures documents. How the two work together, and why MARKDOWN is the one embedded format STXT schemas define.

	Header: @STXT@ vs Markdown

	Assert >>
		Markdown gives format to prose; @STXT@ gives structure to documents.\\
		They solve different problems, and they are designed to be used together.

	Content >>
		This is the one comparison in the series that is not a choice. Markdown answers
		"how do I write bold, links and lists inside a text?"; @STXT@ answers "what are the
		parts of this document, and are they all there?". A README needs the first
		question; a contract, an article pipeline or a corpus of structured records needs
		both.

	Subheader: Where Markdown ends

	Content >>
		Markdown has no notion of fields. The moment a document needs data — an author, a
		date, a status — the ecosystem reaches for **front matter**: a YAML block between
		`---` fences, invisible to Markdown itself and parsed by a second
		language with [its own rules](stxt-vs-yaml):

	Listing >>
		---
		title: Style guide
		author: Ana López
		published: 2026-09-01
		---

		# Style guide

		Write **short** sentences. Prefer [links](https://example.com) to footnotes.

	Content >>
		The structure of that file exists only by convention: nothing checks that
		`published` is a date, that `author` is present, or that the body follows the
		agreed sections. That is not a flaw of Markdown — it never claimed otherwise — but
		it marks the boundary where formatting stops and structure has to begin.

	Subheader: The same document in @STXT@

	Content >>
		In @STXT@ the record is nodes, and the prose is a text block whose type declares it
		as Markdown:

	Code >>
		Article (com.example.article): Style guide
			Author: Ana López
			Published: 2026-09-01
			Body >>
				Write **short** sentences. Prefer [links](https://example.com) to footnotes.

	Content >>
		And the template of its namespace, apart:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.article
			Structure >>
				Article (com.example.article):
					Author: (+)
					Published: (1) DATE
					Body: (1) MARKDOWN

	Content >>
		`MARKDOWN` is the only embedded format the schema layer defines, precisely because
		the two languages divide the work: a node declared with that type marks its text as
		formatted prose, to be interpreted as CommonMark, and the raw form stays readable
		([STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC §9.7](stxt-schema-ref#s9-7)). The validator now guarantees what
		front matter could only suggest: the author is there, the date is a date, the body
		is exactly one block.

	Subheader: A structure Markdown cannot check

	Content >>
		The division scales past one article. This portal is the working example: every
		page of `stxt.dev` — this one included — is an @STXT@ document whose prose blocks
		are Markdown, validated against the site template before a generator renders them,
		as the [CMS and publishing](use-cases-cms) use case describes. Append `.stxt` to
		any page address to read the source.

	Subheader: When to use which

	Content >>
		For a README, a comment, a gist — prose with light formatting and no contract —
		Markdown alone is the right tool, and @STXT@ would add nothing.

		Reach for @STXT@ when the document has parts worth naming and checking:
		[articles in a pipeline](use-cases-cms), [corporate records](use-cases-corporate-docs),
		[technical proposals](use-cases-rfcs). The prose inside stays Markdown; the
		structure around it becomes explicit and validated. The [tutorial](tutorial) covers
		the language in fifteen short sections, the [playground](https://play.stxt.dev)
		runs the example above, and the short version of this page is in the
		[FAQ](faq#why-not-markdown).
